artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a Markov game with a finite set of agents $\mathcal{N} = \{1, \dots, n\}$, state space $\mathcal{S}$, joint action space $\mathcal{A} = \prod_{i=1}^n \mathcal{A}_i$, transition kernel $P(s' \mid s, a)$, and per-agent reward functions $r_i(s, a)$. Suppose we are given an observational dataset $\mathcal{D} = \{(s_t, a_t, r_t, s_{t+1})\}$ generated by a population of trajectories in which:

(i) the data are collected under an unknown mixture of behavior policies $\mu = \sum_k w_k \mu_k$ over the joint action space, with neither the mixture weights $w_k$ nor the component policies $\mu_k$ observed;
(ii) only a subset $\mathcal{O} \subseteq \mathcal{N}$ of agents' actions are recorded, while the actions of the remaining agents in $\mathcal{N} \setminus \mathcal{O}$ are unobserved confounders that may influence both the recorded actions and the environment dynamics;
(iii) a causal directed acyclic graph $\mathcal{G}$ encoding qualitative assumptions about the dependencies among states, observed actions, hidden agents' actions, and rewards is provided, possibly together with sensitivity parameters bounding the strength of unmeasured confounding.

Design a learning procedure that, given $\mathcal{D}$ and $\mathcal{G}$, returns for each evaluation joint policy $\pi$ a confidence interval $[\underline{V}^\pi, \overline{V}^\pi]$ on its expected return $V^\pi = \mathbb{E}_\pi igl[\sum_{t \ge 0} \gamma^t \sum_i r_i(s_t, a_t) igr]$ such that:

(a) the interval is valid (covers $V^\pi$ with prescribed probability) under the stated causal assumptions and the sensitivity bounds on hidden confounding;
(b) the interval is sharp, in the sense that it equals the tightest possible bound implied by the assumptions and the observational distribution;
(c) using these intervals, an offline policy-optimization rule outputs a joint policy $\hat{\pi}$ whose suboptimality with respect to $\max_\pi V^\pi$ admits a finite-sample bound that scales gracefully with the number of agents $n$ and degrades smoothly as the confounding sensitivity parameters grow.

Provide such a procedure together with the corresponding identification, estimation, and regret guarantees, or prove that no such procedure exists under the given assumptions.
